1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,694
Twelve sales, four reps.

2
00:00:01,694 --> 00:00:02,778
Who sold the most?

3
00:00:02,778 --> 00:00:09,640
Instead of sorting, filtering and adding by hand, you type one formula, and Google Sheets builds the whole report.

4
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,927
Every rep, every total, biggest first.

5
00:00:11,927 --> 00:00:15,298
And when a new sale comes in, the report updates itself.

6
00:00:15,998 --> 00:00:20,761
That formula is called QUERY, and it's the most powerful function in Google Sheets.

7
00:00:20,761 --> 00:00:23,631
In the next few minutes you'll learn it from zero.

8
00:00:23,631 --> 00:00:28,968
Picking columns, filtering rows, sorting, totals, and even a pivot table, all in one formula.

9
00:00:28,968 --> 00:00:33,329
Around the middle, I'll show you the one rule that causes most QUERY errors.

10
00:00:34,030 --> 00:00:35,296
QUERY takes three things.

11
00:00:35,296 --> 00:00:36,106
First, the data.

12
00:00:36,106 --> 00:00:38,335
That's your table, including the header row.

13
00:00:38,335 --> 00:00:40,918
Second, the query itself, written in double quotes.

14
00:00:40,918 --> 00:00:45,984
It's a short sentence in a simple language, that says which columns you want and which rows to keep.

15
00:00:45,984 --> 00:00:48,516
And third, the number of header rows, usually one.

16
00:00:48,516 --> 00:00:52,214
You can leave it out, but I always type it, so Sheets never has to guess.

17
00:00:52,914 --> 00:00:54,432
Here's our practice table.

18
00:00:54,432 --> 00:00:59,862
Date in column A, the sales rep in B, the region in C, the product in D, and the amount in E.

19
00:00:59,862 --> 00:01:01,905
Twelve sales, rows two to thirteen.

20
00:01:01,905 --> 00:01:04,766
Type it in, or import any table you already have.

21
00:01:04,766 --> 00:01:07,977
And notice, QUERY talks about columns by their letters.

22
00:01:07,977 --> 00:01:08,795
A, B, C, D, E.

23
00:01:08,795 --> 00:01:09,904
Not by their names.

24
00:01:10,604 --> 00:01:11,300
Let's start.

25
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,124
Click G1 and type equals QUERY, open bracket, A1 colon E13, comma.

26
00:01:15,124 --> 00:01:17,906
Then in double quotes, select B comma D comma E.

27
00:01:17,906 --> 00:01:20,688
Close the quotes, comma, one, close the bracket.

28
00:01:20,688 --> 00:01:21,383
Press Enter.

29
00:01:21,383 --> 00:01:25,672
You get just the rep, the product and the amount, with the headers on top.

30
00:01:25,672 --> 00:01:28,512
Three columns out of five, in any order you want.

31
00:01:29,212 --> 00:01:30,416
Two things to notice.

32
00:01:30,416 --> 00:01:33,170
First, the whole result comes from one cell, G1.

33
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:35,637
It spills down and to the right on its own.

34
00:01:35,637 --> 00:01:38,391
So keep that area empty, or you'll get an error.

35
00:01:38,391 --> 00:01:40,858
And second, select star means every column.

36
00:01:40,858 --> 00:01:41,948
That's the default.

37
00:01:41,948 --> 00:01:45,391
If you leave select out completely, you get the whole table.

38
00:01:46,091 --> 00:01:48,195
And about that last argument, the one.

39
00:01:48,195 --> 00:01:51,074
It tells QUERY your first row is a header, not data.

40
00:01:51,074 --> 00:01:58,825
If you type zero by mistake, the word Amount is treated like a normal value, and your results can come back with blanks or a strange header.

41
00:01:58,825 --> 00:02:02,590
So when your table has one header row, always finish with comma one.

42
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:04,403
Now the useful part.

43
00:02:04,403 --> 00:02:04,960
Filtering.

44
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:06,518
Add where, then a condition.

45
00:02:06,518 --> 00:02:07,631
Where C equals West.

46
00:02:07,631 --> 00:02:09,468
And look carefully at the quotes.

47
00:02:09,468 --> 00:02:14,866
The whole query is in double quotes, so the text you're looking for, West, goes in single quotes.

48
00:02:14,866 --> 00:02:18,595
Press Enter, and you get only the three sales from the West region.

49
00:02:19,295 --> 00:02:20,704
Numbers are even easier.

50
00:02:20,704 --> 00:02:21,702
No quotes at all.

51
00:02:21,702 --> 00:02:23,873
Where E is greater than five hundred.

52
00:02:23,873 --> 00:02:25,987
Four sales, and they're all laptops.

53
00:02:25,987 --> 00:02:30,624
You can use equals, greater than, less than, and greater or less than or equal.

54
00:02:30,624 --> 00:02:34,322
Not equal is written as an exclamation mark and an equals sign.

55
00:02:35,022 --> 00:02:40,350
To combine conditions, just use the words and, and or, the way you'd say it out loud.

56
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:43,234
Where C equals East, and E is at least ninety.

57
00:02:43,234 --> 00:02:43,861
Two sales.

58
00:02:43,861 --> 00:02:47,559
With or, a row only needs one of the conditions to be true.

59
00:02:47,559 --> 00:02:50,192
Where D equals Mouse, or D equals Monitor.

60
00:02:50,192 --> 00:02:50,882
Five sales.

61
00:02:51,582 --> 00:02:54,499
And for text, you're not limited to exact matches.

62
00:02:54,499 --> 00:02:57,534
Contains finds a piece of text anywhere in the cell.

63
00:02:57,534 --> 00:03:00,977
Where D contains top, in single quotes, finds every laptop.

64
00:03:00,977 --> 00:03:03,370
You also have starts with, and ends with.

65
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:03,953
One catch.

66
00:03:03,953 --> 00:03:05,412
These are case sensitive.

67
00:03:05,412 --> 00:03:08,038
Top with a capital T would find nothing here.

68
00:03:08,738 --> 00:03:13,711
Here's the quote rule as a picture, because it's the number one reason a QUERY breaks.

69
00:03:13,711 --> 00:03:15,850
Double quotes around the whole query.

70
00:03:15,850 --> 00:03:17,353
Single quotes around text.

71
00:03:17,353 --> 00:03:18,798
No quotes around numbers.

72
00:03:18,798 --> 00:03:21,227
Get this right, and most errors disappear.

73
00:03:21,927 --> 00:03:22,717
Next, sorting.

74
00:03:22,717 --> 00:03:24,298
Add order by, then a column.

75
00:03:24,298 --> 00:03:28,250
Order by E desc, short for descending, puts the biggest amounts first.

76
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:30,396
Asc, or nothing, means smallest first.

77
00:03:30,396 --> 00:03:33,840
Then add limit three, and you keep only the first three rows.

78
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:35,929
That's a top three list, in one line.

79
00:03:35,929 --> 00:03:37,680
The original table never moves.

80
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:39,459
Now the real magic.

81
00:03:39,459 --> 00:03:39,970
Group by.

82
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:42,413
Select C, comma, sum of E, then group by C.

83
00:03:42,413 --> 00:03:47,070
QUERY puts all the sales from the same region together, and adds up their amounts.

84
00:03:47,070 --> 00:03:48,547
Four regions, four totals.

85
00:03:48,547 --> 00:03:51,331
And the result is sorted by region automatically.

86
00:03:51,331 --> 00:03:53,035
Look at the header it creates.

87
00:03:53,035 --> 00:03:53,660
Sum Amount.

88
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:56,159
The function name, plus the original header.

89
00:03:56,859 --> 00:03:59,432
Sum is one of five functions you can use.

90
00:03:59,432 --> 00:04:02,445
Sum, count, average, written a v g, max and min.

91
00:04:02,445 --> 00:04:04,517
You can even use several at once.

92
00:04:04,517 --> 00:04:07,216
Select B, count of E, max of E, group by B.

93
00:04:07,216 --> 00:04:11,673
Every rep has three sales, and now you can see each one's biggest sale.

94
00:04:12,373 --> 00:04:14,128
Here's the one rule I promised.

95
00:04:14,128 --> 00:04:21,147
When you use group by, every column in select must be either inside a function, like sum or count, or listed after group by.

96
00:04:21,147 --> 00:04:28,223
If you select B and D, but only group by B, QUERY doesn't know which product to show for each rep, and it gives you an error.

97
00:04:28,223 --> 00:04:30,600
Fix it by grouping by both, or removing D.

98
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:33,131
So how does QUERY actually think?

99
00:04:33,131 --> 00:04:34,851
Watch this query, step by step.

100
00:04:34,851 --> 00:04:36,626
It starts with all twelve sales.

101
00:04:36,626 --> 00:04:38,845
Where keeps only sales over one hundred.

102
00:04:38,845 --> 00:04:39,456
Seven left.

103
00:04:39,456 --> 00:04:41,952
Group by puts them together, one row per rep.

104
00:04:41,952 --> 00:04:44,227
Order by sorts the totals, biggest first.

105
00:04:44,227 --> 00:04:46,002
And limit two keeps the top two.

106
00:04:46,002 --> 00:04:46,779
Lena and Omar.

107
00:04:46,779 --> 00:04:49,720
Every clause does one small job, in this exact order.

108
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,509
Here's that same query in the sheet.

109
00:04:52,509 --> 00:04:58,838
Select B, sum of E, where E is greater than one hundred, group by B, order by sum of E descending, limit two.

110
00:04:58,838 --> 00:04:59,534
Press Enter.

111
00:04:59,534 --> 00:05:02,843
Lena with sixteen hundred, and Omar with fourteen ninety.

112
00:05:02,843 --> 00:05:04,759
Exactly what the steps predicted.

113
00:05:04,759 --> 00:05:07,836
The small sales under one hundred were never counted.

114
00:05:08,536 --> 00:05:11,061
And that order matters when you write it, too.

115
00:05:11,061 --> 00:05:13,420
The clauses must always come in this order.

116
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:16,658
Select, where, group by, pivot, order by, limit, and label.

117
00:05:16,658 --> 00:05:19,567
You can skip any of them, but you can't shuffle them.

118
00:05:19,567 --> 00:05:22,475
Put order by before where, and you get a parse error.

119
00:05:23,176 --> 00:05:25,559
Last piece of the report from the start.

120
00:05:25,559 --> 00:05:27,287
Sum Amount is an ugly header.

121
00:05:27,287 --> 00:05:30,564
Add label, sum of E, and the new name in single quotes.

122
00:05:30,564 --> 00:05:30,921
Total.

123
00:05:30,921 --> 00:05:32,351
Now put it all together.

124
00:05:32,351 --> 00:05:37,476
Select B, sum of E, group by B, order by sum of E descending, label sum of E as Total.

125
00:05:37,476 --> 00:05:39,740
Every rep, every total, biggest first.

126
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:41,766
Lena on top, with sixteen seventy.

127
00:05:42,466 --> 00:05:45,488
Want a pivot table, without the pivot table menu?

128
00:05:45,488 --> 00:05:46,968
Add pivot, and a column.

129
00:05:46,968 --> 00:05:49,436
Select B, sum of E, group by B, pivot C.

130
00:05:49,436 --> 00:05:54,679
Now each rep is a row, each region becomes its own column, and every cell is a total.

131
00:05:54,679 --> 00:05:58,010
Empty cells mean that rep had no sales in that region.

132
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:00,416
Lena sold sixteen hundred in the North.

133
00:06:01,116 --> 00:06:02,735
Dates trip up almost everyone.

134
00:06:02,735 --> 00:06:04,841
You can't just type the date in quotes.

135
00:06:04,841 --> 00:06:09,701
You write the word date, then the date in single quotes, as year, month, day, with dashes.

136
00:06:09,701 --> 00:06:12,994
Always that format, no matter how dates look in your country.

137
00:06:12,994 --> 00:06:17,098
Where A is greater than or equal to date, twenty twenty six, eleven, oh one.

138
00:06:17,098 --> 00:06:19,258
Every sale from November, seven of them.

139
00:06:19,958 --> 00:06:21,876
One more thing you'll run into.

140
00:06:21,876 --> 00:06:29,364
If your data isn't a normal range, for example two tabs stacked together inside curly brackets, the letters stop working.

141
00:06:29,364 --> 00:06:34,747
Then you call the columns Col1, Col2, Col3, with a capital C, in the order they appear.

142
00:06:34,747 --> 00:06:37,161
Everything else stays exactly the same.

143
00:06:37,861 --> 00:06:38,213
Bonus.

144
00:06:38,213 --> 00:06:40,267
Make the report change from a cell.

145
00:06:40,267 --> 00:06:42,027
Type a rep's name in H1, Lena.

146
00:06:42,027 --> 00:06:49,244
Then, inside the query, close the double quotes, add an ampersand, H1, another ampersand, and open the double quotes again.

147
00:06:49,244 --> 00:06:52,295
Keep the single quotes around it, because it's text.

148
00:06:52,295 --> 00:06:55,581
Now change H1 to Omar, and the report changes by itself.

149
00:06:55,581 --> 00:06:59,395
Add a dropdown to H1 from the Insert menu, and anyone can use it.

150
00:07:00,095 --> 00:07:00,936
Now the errors.

151
00:07:00,936 --> 00:07:04,639
Unable to parse query string means the query itself has a mistake.

152
00:07:04,639 --> 00:07:09,913
Usually a missing single quote, a comma in the wrong place, or the clauses in the wrong order.

153
00:07:09,913 --> 00:07:14,963
No column means you used a letter outside your data, like G, when the data is only A to E.

154
00:07:14,963 --> 00:07:19,844
And if the result shows hash REF, something is sitting where the results want to spill.

155
00:07:19,844 --> 00:07:20,349
Clear it.

156
00:07:21,049 --> 00:07:23,039
And the sneakiest problem of all.

157
00:07:23,039 --> 00:07:24,849
No error, just missing values.

158
00:07:24,849 --> 00:07:34,619
If a column mixes numbers and text, like a few amounts typed as N A, QUERY treats the whole column as whichever type is most common, and the rest come back empty.

159
00:07:34,619 --> 00:07:36,609
Keep one kind of data per column.

160
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:37,830
Pro tip.

161
00:07:37,830 --> 00:07:41,607
Instead of A1 colon E13, use A colon E, the whole columns.

162
00:07:41,607 --> 00:07:46,035
Then every new sale you add at the bottom is included automatically.

163
00:07:46,035 --> 00:07:50,659
Add where B is not null, so the empty rows below your data are skipped.

164
00:07:50,659 --> 00:07:53,980
Now your report really does update itself, forever.

165
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:55,387
Quick recap.

166
00:07:55,387 --> 00:07:59,574
QUERY takes the data, the query in double quotes, and the header count.

167
00:07:59,574 --> 00:08:00,813
Select picks columns.

168
00:08:00,813 --> 00:08:03,820
Where filters rows, with single quotes around text.

169
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:05,825
Order by and limit make top lists.

170
00:08:05,825 --> 00:08:08,184
Group by with sum or count makes totals.

171
00:08:08,184 --> 00:08:09,482
Label renames headers.

172
00:08:09,482 --> 00:08:11,605
And pivot turns values into columns.

173
00:08:11,605 --> 00:08:13,846
Pause and screenshot this cheat sheet.

174
00:08:14,545 --> 00:08:20,821
Next on Shortcut Lab, we build your first web page, with HTML and CSS, in fifteen minutes.

175
00:08:20,821 --> 00:08:22,285
No experience needed.

176
00:08:22,285 --> 00:08:24,517
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
